# 4. Coding Standards

## 4.1 HTML

- Use HTML5 semantic elements.
- Use meaningful page titles.
- Include appropriate meta descriptions.
- Use descriptive class names.
- Maintain consistent indentation.
- Avoid duplicated markup where practical.
- Include appropriate image alt attributes.
- Ensure valid and accessible HTML structure.

Prefer semantic elements such as:

```html
<header>
<nav>
<main>
<section>
<article>
<footer>
```

Avoid using generic div elements for everything.

## 4.2 CSS

- Organize styles logically.
- Use CSS variables for brand colors.
- Avoid unnecessary specificity.
- Avoid excessive !important declarations.
- Maintain consistent spacing.
- Use reusable component styles.
- Prevent style duplication.
- Keep responsive rules organized.

Example:

```css
:root {
    --color-charcoal: #1F2328;
    --color-background: #F5F5F5;
    --color-grey: #B5B5B5;
    --color-gold: #C9A66B;
    --color-white: #FFFFFF;
}
```

Build reusable styles for:

- Buttons.
- Navigation.
- Product cards.
- Collection sections.
- Typography.
- Containers.
- Footer.
- Interactive elements.

## 4.3 JavaScript

Use modern Vanilla JavaScript.

Standards:

- Use const and let.
- Prefer modular and reusable functions.
- Avoid unnecessary global variables.
- Use event listeners properly.
- Validate elements before interacting with them.
- Avoid duplicate event listeners.
- Handle errors appropriately.
- Keep product data separate from presentation logic where practical.
- Avoid unnecessary JavaScript dependencies.

Do not use JavaScript for functionality that can be implemented more efficiently with HTML or CSS.

# 5. Design System Implementation

Implement the visual direction defined in Document 01.

Maintain consistent:

- Colors.
- Typography.
- Spacing.
- Border radii.
- Buttons.
- Image presentation.
- Navigation.
- Product card layouts.
- Hover and focus states.

Create a coherent design system before building multiple pages.

Do not introduce random colors or unrelated component styles.

Use gold as a restrained accent rather than the dominant color.

# 6. User Interface Requirements

## 6.1 Navigation

Implement:

- Desktop navigation.
- Responsive mobile navigation.
- Active navigation states.
- Hover and focus states.
- Functional page links.
- Accessible mobile menu controls.

Use Bootstrap components where they provide genuine value.

## 6.2 Product Cards

All product cards should have a consistent structure.

A card may contain:

- Product image.
- Product name.
- Product category.
- Available color information.
- Short description.
- Product details link.
- WhatsApp inquiry action.

Do not invent product prices, sizes, availability or descriptions.

Only display information that has been provided or verified.

## 6.3 Product Collections

Keep product information organized separately from HTML presentation when practical.

Use a lightweight JavaScript data structure for product information if it simplifies maintenance.

Avoid building a complicated product management system.

## 6.4 WhatsApp Integration

Create a centralized configuration for the official WhatsApp number.

Example placeholder:

```javascript
const SITE_CONFIG = {
    whatsappNumber: "REPLACE_WITH_NAZURI_WHATSAPP_NUMBER"
};
```

Do not invent or publish a fake phone number.

Do not expose sensitive information in frontend configuration.

# 7. Responsive Development

Use a mobile-first approach.

Test the website at representative viewport widths:

- 320px.
- 375px.
- 425px.
- 768px.
- 1024px.
- 1440px.

Verify:

- Navigation behavior.
- Product grid arrangement.
- Image dimensions.
- Text wrapping.
- Button sizes.
- Section spacing.
- Footer arrangement.
- Horizontal overflow.

Never assume a website is responsive simply because Bootstrap is installed.

Test the actual implementation.

# 8. Image and Asset Management

Product images are supplied by the project owner.

Requirements:

- Preserve original product photography.
- Use local assets.
- Optimize image sizes.
- Use appropriate image formats.
- Avoid unnecessary duplicate images.
- Use descriptive filenames.
- Specify image dimensions or aspect ratios where appropriate.
- Use lazy loading for suitable below-the-fold images.
- Avoid lazy loading the main hero image when it would delay the initial visual presentation.

Do not download random images from the internet to fill product sections.

If an image is missing, use a clear placeholder rather than inventing product photography.

# 9. Performance Optimization

The website must be lightweight.

Apply:

- Optimized images.
- Efficient CSS.
- Efficient JavaScript.
- Minimal external dependencies.
- Appropriate browser caching through hosting configuration.
- Lazy loading where suitable.
- Avoidance of unnecessary animation.
- Avoidance of unnecessary network requests.

Avoid loading multiple libraries that perform the same task.

# 10. SEO and Discoverability

Implement basic search engine optimization.

Each page must have:

- A unique title.
- A meaningful meta description.
- Appropriate heading hierarchy.
- Descriptive image alt text.
- Semantic HTML.
- Relevant internal links.
- Canonical URL when the final production domain is known.

Also prepare:

- Favicon.
- Open Graph metadata.
- Social sharing image when supplied.
- robots.txt when appropriate.
- sitemap.xml when the production URL is established.

Do not invent a production domain.

# 11. Accessibility

Follow practical WCAG accessibility principles.

Ensure:

- Keyboard-accessible navigation.
- Visible focus states.
- Sufficient text contrast.
- Descriptive labels.
- Appropriate alt text.
- Accessible interactive controls.
- Correct button and link semantics.
- Respect for reduced-motion preferences.

Do not communicate information through color alone.

# 12. Security

Even though this is a static website:

- Avoid inserting untrusted content through unsafe innerHTML.
- Validate and encode dynamic content appropriately.
- Avoid exposing secrets or private tokens.
- Do not store sensitive customer information in frontend code.
- Use HTTPS in production.
- Use trusted dependencies.
- Avoid unnecessary third-party scripts.

# 13. Browser Compatibility

Test using current versions of:

- Google Chrome.
- Microsoft Edge.
- Firefox where available.

Verify both desktop and mobile layouts.

