- Review the complete project.
- Commit verified changes.
- Push to GitHub.
- Configure static hosting.
- Test the deployed website.
- Verify production links and metadata.

# 15. Git and Version Control Rules

Use Git throughout development.

Recommended commit messages:

```text
Initial project setup
Build responsive navigation
Implement homepage hero section
Add product collection layouts
Implement product inquiry actions
Improve mobile responsiveness
Optimize images and performance
Prepare production deployment
```

Rules:

- Commit meaningful changes.
- Avoid committing temporary files.
- Never commit passwords or secrets.
- Do not force-push without a justified reason.
- Test before committing major functionality.
- Keep the main branch stable.
- Use feature branches when working on substantial independent changes.

# 16. AI Coding Agent Instructions

Any AI coding agent assisting with this project must follow these rules.

1. Read both project documents before implementing changes.
2. Inspect the existing project structure before modifying files.
3. Understand the requested task before writing code.
4. Do not redesign approved sections without permission.
5. Do not introduce unnecessary frameworks.
6. Do not create unnecessary dependencies.
7. Do not invent business information.
8. Do not invent product information.
9. Do not invent contact details.
10. Do not download product photographs without authorization.
11. Do not remove working functionality without justification.
12. Maintain consistent design patterns.
13. Test changes after implementation.
14. Report files created or modified.
15. Explain important technical decisions.
16. Report unresolved issues honestly.

When implementing a feature, modify only the files necessary for that feature.

Avoid replacing entire files when a targeted modification is sufficient.

If an implementation decision could significantly change the brand experience or project architecture, request approval before proceeding.

# 17. Definition of Done

A feature is complete only when:

- Its intended functionality works.
- Its design follows the established system.
- It is responsive.
- It has no known critical console errors.
- It does not break existing functionality.
- Its links and buttons work.
- Its implementation is maintainable.
- Its relevant changes have been tested.

The complete website is ready for deployment only after a final review of all pages and functionality.

